CONCEPT: Window of Exposure
----------------------------
The "window of exposure" is the time between when a vulnerability is
publicly disclosed and when the organization successfully remediates it.

  Disclosure Date ----[WINDOW OF EXPOSURE]----> Remediation Date

Per ACS Corp policy, maximum acceptable windows:
  Critical (CVSS 9.0+):   7 calendar days
  High (CVSS 7.0-8.9):   14 calendar days
  Medium (CVSS 4.0-6.9): 30 calendar days
  Low (CVSS 0.1-3.9):    90 calendar days

TIMELINE EXAMPLE:
-----------------
Jan 15 - CVE-2026-1234 disclosed (CVSS 9.3 Critical)
Jan 15 - Vendor releases patch
Jan 16 - CISA advisory published
Jan 18 - ACS vulnerability scan detects finding
Jan 20 - Remediation ticket created (IT-2026-0042)
Jan 22 - Change window scheduled
Feb 02 - Patch applied to {{ prefix }}-ACS-SRV-002
Feb 02 - Vulnerability confirmed resolved

Window of Exposure: 18 days
Policy Threshold:    7 days (Critical)
Status:             NON-COMPLIANT (exceeded by 11 days)

FACTORS THAT INCREASE EXPOSURE:
-------------------------------
- Delayed scanning cadence
- Backlogged change advisory boards
- Failed patch attempts counted as "in progress"
- Lack of compensating controls during exposure window
- No interim risk acceptance documentation

